    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.union - EdgeCase\", \"isPartOf\": {\"@type\":\n    \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.union.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.union()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label .doc-label-method}\n    | EdgeCase\n  description: Compute the union of this graph with another graph.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.union()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label .doc-label-method}\n    | EdgeCase\n  description: Compute the union of this graph with another graph.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.union()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: Compute the union of this graph with another graph.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nCompute the union of this graph with another graph.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L49321-L49435){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.union(other)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Union](../assets/graphs/union.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"G has edges A–B and B–D, which H shares, plus A–C (only in G, accent). H adds C–D (only in H, teal). The union keeps all four nodes and all four edges, each in the color of the graph it came from.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nReturns a new graph containing all nodes and edges from both graphs. Nodes with the same ID\nare merged, whatever their labels; where both graphs give the same node (or edge) a label,\nattribute, or weight, this graph's value takes precedence and the other graph fills in the\nrest. Duplicate edges are not added.\n\nTwo edges count as the same edge when they join the same pair of node IDs (in the same\ndirection, for directed graphs). Union is the operation to reach for when two graphs\ndescribe parts of the same network and you want everything in one place, for example:\n\n- combining snapshots of a network taken at different times\n- merging edges from several data sources (e.g., interactions reported by different\n  experiments) over a shared set of node IDs\n- overlaying one relationship type on another, such as \"works with\" and \"emails\"\n\nBecause nodes are matched by ID, the two graphs must use a shared numbering for the same\nentities. If the numbering differs, use `disjoint_union()` (to keep everything separate)\nor rebuild one graph with matching IDs first. Isolated nodes from both graphs are kept.\nEdges from this graph keep their IDs; edges only `other` has get fresh IDs above every\nID this graph has used. Neither input graph is modified. To let the other graph's\nvalues win instead, use `compose()`.\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[other]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   The graph to union with. It must have the same directedness as this graph. Its nodes\n    are matched to this graph's nodes by ID; nodes and edges it has that this graph lacks\n    are added, and for nodes and edges present in both, its labels, attributes, and\n    weights are used only where this graph has no value of its own.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   A new graph containing all nodes and edges from both graphs. It is weighted if either\n    input is weighted.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If the graphs have different directedness (one directed, one undirected), or if the\n    same label belongs to different node IDs in the two graphs.\n:::\n\n## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}\n\nUnion takes the pointwise superset of nodes and edges, merging any nodes that share\nan ID; where attributes clash, the calling graph wins. Both operands must agree on\ndirectedness, because there is no principled way to fold a directed edge into an\nundirected graph without losing information. If either operand is weighted, so is\nthe result. Use `disjoint_union()` when you want to keep node IDs strictly separate.\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~compose`) \n\n:   Combine two graphs, letting the other graph's values win.\n\n[](`~disjoint_union`) \n\n:   Combine two graphs, keeping their nodes apart.\n\n[](`~union_all`) \n\n:   Combine any number of graphs.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nNodes are matched by ID, not by label. When both graphs have the same node or edge, this\ngraph's values win and the other graph only fills gaps. Node \"A\" keeps `team=\"red\"` from\n`g1` but gains `city=\"Oslo\"` from `g2`, and the shared edge keeps `g1`'s weight of `1.0`:\n\n::: {#a944fa72 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ng1 = ec.Graph()\ng1.add_nodes([(\"A\", {\"team\": \"red\"}), \"B\"])\ng1.add_edge(\"A\", \"B\", weight=1.0)\n\ng2 = ec.Graph()\ng2.add_nodes([(\"A\", {\"team\": \"blue\", \"city\": \"Oslo\"}), \"B\", \"C\"])\ng2.add_edges([(\"A\", \"B\", 5.0), (\"B\", \"C\")])\n\nmerged = g1.union(g2)\nmerged.get_node_df().select(\"label\", \"team\", \"city\")\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```{=html}\n<div><style>\n.dataframe > thead > tr,\n.dataframe > tbody > tr {\n  text-align: right;\n  white-space: pre-wrap;\n}\n</style>\n<small>shape: (3, 3)</small><table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\"><thead><tr><th>label</th><th>team</th><th>city</th></tr><tr><td>str</td><td>str</td><td>str</td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>&quot;A&quot;</td><td>&quot;red&quot;</td><td>&quot;Oslo&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;B&quot;</td><td>null</td><td>null</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;C&quot;</td><td>null</td><td>null</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n::: {#ee660ea8 .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nmerged.get_edge_df().select(\"source\", \"target\", \"weight\")\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```{=html}\n<div><style>\n.dataframe > thead > tr,\n.dataframe > tbody > tr {\n  text-align: right;\n  white-space: pre-wrap;\n}\n</style>\n<small>shape: (2, 3)</small><table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\"><thead><tr><th>source</th><th>target</th><th>weight</th></tr><tr><td>i64</td><td>i64</td><td>f64</td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>2</td><td>1.0</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>3</td><td>null</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### Two club lists\n\nIn a network of friendships among the members of a chess club and a climbing club, each club\nkeeps a list of the friendships among its own members. `subgraph()` keeps the network's node\nIDs, which is what lets `union()` match the two lists up. Their union has all 24 members and\nthe 38 friendships within a club, but not the 5 between the clubs, which neither list holds:\n\n::: {#e3f22140 .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nfriends = ec.friends()\nnodes = friends.get_node_df()\n\n\ndef club(name):\n    ids = nodes.filter(club=name)[\"id\"].to_list()\n    return friends.subgraph(ids)\n\n\nlists = club(\"Chess\").union(club(\"Climbing\"))\nlists.count_edges(), friends.count_edges() - lists.count_edges()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```\n(38, 5)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n::: {#1e83a7fe .cell execution_count=4}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nlists.plot(color_by=\"club\", label_by=\"label\", seed=2)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=4}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}}:is(html.quarto-light, body.quarto-light, html[data-bs-theme=\"light\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"true\"], body.vscode-light, body.vscode-high-contrast-light, html[theme=\"light\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}:is(html.quarto-dark, body.quarto-dark, html[data-bs-theme=\"dark\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"false\"], body.vscode-dark, body.vscode-high-contrast:not(.vscode-high-contrast-light), html[theme=\"dark\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}</style><div class=\"ec-plot\" style=\"position:relative;width:fit-content;max-width:100%;margin:1em auto\">\n<style>.ec-plot > svg { display:block; max-width:100%; height:auto; }</style>\n<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"700\" height=\"500\" viewBox=\"55.8 14.0 688.8 492.0\" style=\"font-family: -apple-system, Bl